    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d230-a4">

                <pb facs="0376" n="282" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg282"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 334.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Vignaud to Mr.
                            Walker</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-12-24"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 24,
                        1886</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: Referring to my letter of the 16th
                    instant, I am directed by the minister to say that in the matter of papers
                    required by the French authorities for the marriage of American citizens in
                    France, it is desirable that all such papers be issued by the legation directly
                    to the parties interested. The propriety of this rule comes from the
                    consideration that the original understanding with the foreign office concerning
                    these marriages contemplated only papers bearing the seal of the legation, and
                    any other course may create confusion and perhaps bring into question the
                    practice itself.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry Vignaud.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
